WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.780
A signal has a disposition: what the process will do when that signal is delivered.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:12.760
Some signals have default actions such as terminating the process, stopping it, continuing it, or being ignored.

3
00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:19.820
A program can install a handler for selected signals, ignore selected signals, or restore default behavior where appropriate.

4
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.080
These choices should be part of the design, not scattered setup code.

5
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:28.520
Installed handlers change the program's behavior under interruption.

6
00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:36.460
A handler might request shutdown, notify an event loop, record a simple state change, or coordinate child process cleanup through normal code.

7
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.560
The key point is that the handler runs asynchronously relative to the interrupted code.

8
00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.900
It should be treated as a restricted entry point, not as a normal callback.

9
00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:52.380
Signal masks control whether selected signals are blocked for a thread or process context.

10
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.680
When a signal is blocked, delivery may be delayed and the signal can become pending.

11
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.940
This is useful during sensitive setup or state transitions, but it also creates design responsibilities.

12
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:10.120
The program should restore masks predictably and avoid leaving signals blocked by accident.

13
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.120
Delivery behavior is platform-sensitive.

14
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:28.100
POSIX-style systems have concepts such as process-directed signals, thread-directed signals, pending signals, signal masks, interrupted system calls, process groups, and child process notifications.

15
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:33.560
Other platforms may use different mechanisms such as console control handlers or service-control callbacks.

16
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.460
A portable design avoids assuming that one environment's details apply everywhere.

17
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:42.180
Interrupted operations also need explicit handling.

18
00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.280
Some blocking calls may return early when a signal is handled.

19
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:52.300
Some library or system calls may be restartable depending on configuration, platform, and call type.

20
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:02.400
Code that waits, reads, writes, accepts connections, or coordinates child processes should define how interruption is reported and how normal processing resumes or shuts down.

21
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.840
A reviewer should be able to map the signal flow from configuration to runtime behavior.

22
00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:08.380
Which signals are handled?

23
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.320
Which are blocked during setup?

24
00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:13.700
Which thread or loop observes shutdown requests?

25
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.640
Which operations may be interrupted?

26
00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:21.180
This map is what turns signal handling from hidden control flow into maintainable engineering.
